From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 18:45:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] Answer unmapped guest addresses with open bus, not a fault

Emulating a guest access means touching the linear alias of the guest
address space. When the guest address is one the machine does not have --
a pointer went wild, or software is sizing memory -- that access faults
*inside the fault handler*, which re-enters itself. 512 KB of ARM stack is
gone in a few thousand turns and the machine hangs with its PC parked on
the exception vector.

Measured, not hypothetical: three stalls out of three in one series, all
first faulting on a guest access, once to 0x64656275 -- the bytes "debu",
a pointer to the string "debug" being used as an address.

Predicting which addresses are backed does not work, and two attempts have
failed at it. A table of regions rejected the framebuffer at 0x3c100000
along with everything else outside sys_memory, and the machine booted
perfectly with nothing ever appearing on screen. Asking the MMU with
`at s1e1r` does not help either: the alias is mapped as one blanket range,
so translation always succeeds. The abort comes from the bus, not the page
tables, and arrives as ESR DFSC 0x10 -- a synchronous external abort.

So do not predict. Recover. Catch that abort where it lands, give the guest
what a 68k board gives an unclaimed address -- all ones on a read, nothing
on a write -- step over the instruction that faulted, and carry on. Nothing
has to know the memory map, and a backed address costs nothing: there is no
test on the fast path.

Guarded twice over: only while already inside SYSHandler, and only for an
abort in the alias window. An external abort anywhere else keeps its old
behaviour, which is to be reported as the real fault it is.

This does not fix the guest going wild. It makes that failure survivable and
reportable instead of destroying the emulator that would have to diagnose it.

The report carries the guest PC, which is what makes it useful: Emu68 keeps the
m68k context in a reserved register, and the AROS image loads at a known base,
so the address resolves against the ELF. That is how the persistent
0xc0000058 access was finally attributed -- to tlsf_freevec, the allocator's
free path, following a pointer that is not one.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/src/aarch64/vectors.c
+++ b/src/aarch64/vectors.c
@@ -317,6 +317,79 @@
     INTREQR = 0xDFF01E,
 };
 
+/*
+ * Open bus.
+ *
+ * A guest access that reaches the fall-through in SYSWriteValToAddr() or
+ * SYSReadValFromAddr() is emulated by touching the linear alias of the guest
+ * address space at 0xffffff9000000000. When the guest address is one the
+ * machine does not have -- because a pointer went wild, or because software is
+ * sizing memory -- that alias is not mapped, the access faults *inside the
+ * fault handler*, and the handler re-enters itself. 512 KB of ARM stack is gone
+ * in a few thousand turns and the machine hangs with a PC parked on the vector.
+ *
+ * A real 68k board answers an unclaimed address with open bus, or raises a bus
+ * error the guest can trap. Either is better than destroying the emulator.
+ *
+ * Predicting which addresses are backed does not work, and two attempts have
+ * now failed at it. A table of regions rejected the framebuffer at 0x3c100000
+ * along with everything else outside `sys_memory`, and the machine booted
+ * perfectly with nothing ever appearing on screen. Asking the MMU with
+ * `at s1e1r` does not help either: the alias is mapped as one blanket range, so
+ * translation always succeeds -- the abort comes from the bus, not the page
+ * tables, and arrives as ESR DFSC 0x10, a synchronous external abort.
+ *
+ * So do not predict. Recover: catch that abort where it lands, hand the guest
+ * open bus, step over the instruction that faulted and carry on. Nothing has to
+ * know the memory map, and an address that is backed costs nothing at all --
+ * there is no test on the fast path.
+ */
+/*
+ * Report, but do not drown. A guest looping over unmapped addresses -- memory
+ * sizing does exactly that -- would otherwise turn the serial console into the
+ * new bottleneck. The first few carry the information; the count is what says
+ * whether it was one wild pointer or a sweep.
+ */
+static void open_bus_report(const char *what, uint64_t far, int size, uint64_t value)
+{
+    static uint32_t seen = 0;
+    struct M68KState *m68k;
+
+    /* The guest PC is what identifies the code doing this. Emu68 keeps the
+     * m68k context in a reserved register, and the AROS image loads at a known
+     * base, so the address resolves against the ELF this repository builds. */
+    __asm__ volatile("mov %0, "CTX_POINTER_ASM"\n":"=r"(m68k));
+
+    seen++;
+    if (seen <= 8)
+    {
+        /*
+         * The guest's A7 lives in an ARM register while the JIT runs
+         * (REG_A7 in M68k.h), so read it from there rather than from the
+         * saved context, which is stale here. The longword at the top of the
+         * stack is the return address of a leaf call -- which is what
+         * identifies the *caller* passing the bad pointer, where m68kPC only
+         * names the victim.
+         *
+         * Guarded by range: reading through the alias is what open bus exists
+         * to survive, and doing it from inside the open-bus path would
+         * recurse.
+         */
+        uint32_t a7 = 0, ret = 0;
+        __asm__ volatile("mov %w0, w29\n":"=r"(a7));
+        if (a7 >= 4 && a7 < 0x34000000)
+            ret = *(volatile uint32_t *)(0xffffff9000000000ULL + a7);
+
+        kprintf("[JIT:SYS] open bus %s: guest %p size %d value %p m68kPC %08x"
+                " A7 %08x ret %08x (#%d)\n",
+                what, far, size, value, m68k ? m68k->PC : 0, a7, ret, seen);
+    }
+    else if (seen == 9)
+    {
+        kprintf("[JIT:SYS] open bus: further reports suppressed\n");
+    }
+}
+
 #ifdef PISTORM_ANY_MODEL
 
 #include "ps_protocol.h"
@@ -2336,6 +2409,41 @@
     static volatile uint32_t sys_depth[4];
     static volatile uint32_t sys_reported[4];
 
+    /*
+     * Open bus, caught where it actually lands.
+     *
+     * This is the emulation of a guest access having faulted on the linear
+     * alias -- a guest address the machine does not have. ESR DFSC 0x10 is a
+     * synchronous external abort, which is what unassigned physical space
+     * answers with; the page tables mapped it fine, so no translation check
+     * could have seen this coming.
+     *
+     * Give the guest what a 68k board gives an unclaimed address: all ones on a
+     * read, nothing on a write. Then step over the instruction that faulted and
+     * return. The destination register is taken from the opcode -- bit 22
+     * distinguishes load from store and the low five bits are Rt, which covers
+     * the plain loads and stores the fall-through emits.
+     *
+     * Only inside SYSHandler, and only in the alias window. An external abort
+     * anywhere else is a real fault and must keep its old behaviour.
+     */
+    if (sys_depth[cpu_id] != 0 &&
+        (esr & 0xf8000000) == 0x90000000 && (esr & 0x3f) == 0x10 &&
+        far >= 0xffffff9000000000 && far < 0xffffff9100000000)
+    {
+        uint32_t opcode = LE32(*(uint32_t *)elr);
+        uint64_t guest = far - 0xffffff9000000000;
+
+        open_bus_report((opcode & (1 << 22)) ? "read" : "write", guest, 0, 0);
+
+        if (opcode & (1 << 22))
+            SYSPutValueToReg(~(uint64_t)0, opcode & 31, ctx);
+
+        elr += 4;
+        __asm__ volatile("msr ELR_EL1, %0"::"r"(elr));
+        return;
+    }
+
     if (sys_depth[cpu_id] != 0)
     {
         if (sys_reported[cpu_id] == 0)
